 "text": "Right now on a bare rock in the Pacific, this sea bird is being drained, one drop at a time. Not by a shark, not by a storm, but by a bird small enough to sit in the palm of your hand. And out here, there is nowhere left to run. It has tried everything, shaking them off, snapping, twisting away. They simply hop back on. Every hour a little more of its blood is carried off, and the great bird just endures it. As if being slowly emptied were somehow the safer choice. This is a groundfinch. A seed eater. The same unremarkable little bird Darwin watched cracking seeds on these islands two centuries ago. It has no business wanting blood at all. So how did a seed eater learn to drink blood? And why does a sea bird 20 times its size simply let it happen? It works the base of a growing feather where the blood runs closest to the skin. And for the first time in days the sea bird goes perfectly still. It did not begin as cruelty. Long ago these finches earned their place on the boobies' backs. Picking off the ticks and biting flies the big birds could never reach themselves. A fair trade. The sea bird got clean, the finch got fed. But every so often a beak broke skin. A bead of blood welled up and the finch drank. And a bird starving on a bare rock does not forget a taste like that. Slowly over generations the cleaner became the parasite. Today close to a tenth of everything this finch eats is blood. It still cannot make its own water. It still cannot cross the open sea. Everything it needs it must take from the only large warm living thing for miles. Because these are two of the most remote islands in all the Galapagos. Wolf and Darwin. No streams, no soft ground. For half of every year the rain simply stops and the seeds run out. So the finch drinks whatever it can find. Nectar from a cactus flower, sea water, even the droppings of other birds. And when there is nothing else left at all there is always blood. On an island full of sea birds it is the one thing that never runs dry. But there is a deeper reason the sea bird will not simply stand up and leave. Beneath it in the dust is a single egg. And these same finches are egg thieves. The instant the booby lifts off to escape the bleeding they take the very thing it was trying to protect. So it makes the only choice the island allows. It stays, it shields the egg, and it lets them drink. By morning it is quiet again. The finches settle back on to the bird they feed on. As though nothing had passed between them. The sea bird keeps its egg. The finch keeps its life. Out here at the far edge of the world survival is not kindness. And it is not cruelty. It is only the bargain the island allows. Paid one drop of blood at a time.",
